Task 1: Creating Directories Using mkdir
Subtask 1.1: Create a Single Directory
Open a terminal.
Use the mkdir command to create a directory named lab_files:
mkdir lab_files
Verify the directory was created:
ls
Expected Output: lab_files should appear in the list of directories.
Subtask 1.2: Create Nested Directories
Create a nested directory structure (parent/child/grandchild) using the -p flag:
mkdir -p parent/child/grandchild
Verify the structure:
tree parent
Expected Output:
parent/
└── child
    └── grandchild
Task 2: Moving, Copying, and Deleting Files
Subtask 2.1: Move Files Using mv
Create a sample file:
touch sample.txt
Move the file to the lab_files directory:
mv sample.txt lab_files/
Verify the file was moved:
ls lab_files/
Expected Output: sample.txt should appear in lab_files.
Subtask 2.2: Copy Files Using cp
Copy sample.txt to a new file named sample_copy.txt:
cp lab_files/sample.txt lab_files/sample_copy.txt
Verify the copy:
ls lab_files/
Expected Output: Both sample.txt and sample_copy.txt should appear.
Subtask 2.3: Delete Files Using rm
Delete sample_copy.txt:
rm lab_files/sample_copy.txt
Verify the deletion:
ls lab_files/
Expected Output: Only sample.txt should remain.
Task 3: Advanced Directory Management
Subtask 3.1: Delete Directories Using rmdir and rm
Attempt to delete the grandchild directory (empty directory):
rmdir parent/child/grandchild
Delete the entire parent directory (including non-empty subdirectories):
rm -r parent
Note: The -r flag recursively deletes directories and their contents.
Subtask 3.2: Wildcard Operations
Create multiple test files:
touch lab_files/file{1..5}.txt
Delete all .txt files in lab_files:
rm lab_files/*.txt
Expected Output: The lab_files directory should be empty.
